    desc: "Born from pre-Liberation film debates and ignited by the end of the war, Italian neorealism took cameras out of Cinecitta into streets stripped by bombing. Rossellini's Rome, Open City (1945) and De Sica's Bicycle Thieves (1948) mixed non-professional actors, location sound and loose episodic plots with working-class protagonists for whom poverty, not romance, drives the story. Screenwriter Cesare Zavattini pushed the doctrine that a film should need nothing invented, while Visconti's La Terra Trema shot an entire Sicilian fishing village in dialect. Stock shortages, black markets and censors shaped the look as much as ideology did. By the early 1950s the box office pulled production back to spectacle, yet the style became the century's template for realist cinema from Satyajit Ray to the Dardennes.",
