    desc: "A generation of cinephile critics mounted the auteur policy — the director as author — and then proved it with cheap, fast, personal films. Truffaut's The 400 Blows (1959) and Godard's Breathless (1960) abandoned studio polish for handheld location shooting, direct sound, jump cuts and open endings, quoting American genre movies with ironic tenderness. The Left Bank strand — Agnes Varda's Cleo from 5 to 7 (1962), Resnais's Hiroshima mon amour — braided essay, memory and documentary. Budgets were tiny, crews skeletal, and self-reference part of the grammar. The wave remade world financing and festival culture, scattered into May 68 politics, and remains the default image of what it means to make a personal film.",
