    sum: "A school that discovered meaning lives between shots — the Kuleshov effect, Odessa Steps, and a city made to dance for the camera.",
    desc: "With film stock scarce after the revolution, the Kuleshov workshop re-edited existing footage and proved that meaning is manufactured in the join: the same neutral face read as grief or appetite beside different images. Eisenstein theorised montage as collision — intellectual, rhythmic, tonal — and staged the Odessa Steps massacre in Battleship Potemkin (1925) as a machine of shock cuts. Vertov's Man with a Movie Camera (1929) turned editing itself into the hero, exposing splices, cranks and city symphony rhythms. Designers from the constructivist wing supplied aggressive diagonals and photomontage graphics. The school was formally retired by the 1934 socialist realism decree, but its grammar passed into advertising, propaganda and every film school curriculum.",
