    desc: "A cluster of young studio rebels — Nagisa Oshima, Yoshishige Yoshida, Masahiro Shinoda at Shochiku, Seijun Suzuki at Nikkatsu — gave Japanese cinema its own New Wave, the Nuberu bagu. Cruel Story of Youth (1960) and The Sun's Burial mapped postwar delinquency onto a country bursting from prosperity into rage; Suzuki's Branded to Kill (1967) flattened the yakuza picture into pop-art anarchy until the studio fired him and students protested. Films interrogated the emperor system, Korean diaspora and student left, favouring wipe cuts, frontal staging, saturated color and black comedy. The Art Theatre Guild gave the movement a second, freer home, and Toshio Matsumoto's Funeral Parade of Roses (1969) fed straight into global counterculture before the movement dissolved into independents around 1972.",
