    sum: "Between Bonnie and Clyde and Heaven's Gate, young American directors turned the studio film gritty, personal and ambiguous.",
    desc: "As the Production Code collapsed and the old audience defected to television, a new generation seized the means: Bonnie and Clyde (1967), Easy Rider (1969) and MASH (1970) proved outsiders could hit big on location with ambiguous heroes and rock soundtracks. Through the decade Coppola, Scorsese, Altman, Bogdanovich, Friedkin and Malick worked loose, grainy realism — zoom lenses, available dark, offhand profanity, downbeat endings like Chinatown (1974). Personal production companies and presold soundtracks reshaped the business, until Jaws (1975) and Star Wars (1977) built the blockbuster machine and the era closed with the costly failures of 1980. Its look — Kodak amber, dirty skylines, antiheroes in hats — still defines the idea of serious American film.",
