    sum: "A ten-rule 'Vow of Chastity' that banned lights, props and gimmicks — and accidentally birthed the handheld lo-fi revolution.",
    desc: "Announced in Paris in 1995 with red prospectuses, the Dogme 95 manifesto by Lars von Trier and Thomas Vinterberg demanded location shooting, no imported props, no artificial light, no filters, no superficial action, no genre trappings and no on-screen credits, signed as a Vow of Chastity. Vinterberg's Festen (1998) and von Trier's The Idiots (1998) proved the rules could detonate melodrama; 35 numbered certificates followed, from Italian for Beginners (2000) to 2005, with confessing directors trading rule-breaks in secret annexes. The washed video-grain look read as truth in an era of CGI overload and seeded found-footage and smartphone-native realism. The collective disbanded quietly, but its certificate system remains cinema's most famous aesthetic contract.",
