    cat: "Film & Cinema", c: "action genre tradition", e: "historical", o: "Shaw Brothers studio system and the kung fu revival; John Woo's 1980s heroic bloodshed",
    g: "Hong Kong", p: "1960s-1990s (golden age 1985-1994)", era: "studio era to handover era",
    sum: "Balletic kung fu, gun-fu melodrama and stuntmen flying through plate glass — the most influential action grammar ever built.",
    desc: "Hong Kong industrialised screen violence: Shaw Brothers' swordplay epics like The One-Armed Swordsman (1967) trained a stunt ecosystem, the kung fu boom of the early 70s globalised it, and Jackie Chan's Police Story (1985) fused slapstick with real, insured-by-nobody falls. John Woo's heroic bloodshed — A Better Tomorrow (1986), The Killer, Hard Boiled — remade the gangster film as gun-melodrama with doves, trench coats and two-fisted loyalty codes, cut with the island's famous speed: shoot first, pre-plan everything, and let the cameraman be a stuntman. Tsui Hark's wirework fantasy and category-III grit filled out the scene until the 1997 handover drained talent to Hollywood, where The Matrix openly bought the grammar whole.",
    col: [{ h: "#1d2733", n: "midnight blue" }, { h: "#e0a13c", n: "sodium orange" }, { h: "#b3342c", n: "temple red" }, { h: "#e8e3d5", n: "nightclub white" }, { h: "#2e6e5a", n: "jade green" }],
