    sum: "Chopped-and-screwed hip-hop slowed into occult fog, branded with triangles, Unicode and MySpace-era black magic.",
    desc: "Witch house was a 2009-2013 internet microgenre that pitched slowed, chopped-and-screwed hip-hop behind haze-drenched synths and screamed-through-reverb vocals, distributed as MP3s across Tumblr, SoundCloud and Last.fm rather than labels. Salem's King Night (2010) and Pictureplane's Dark Rift (2009) anchored the canon while oOoOO, White Ring and Ritualz spread the fog; fans and artists coded the look with triangles, crosses, inverted symbols and Unicode names like ▲. The alias 'drag' and 'screwgaze' circulated alongside; critics doubted its coherence and the scene flamed out by 2013. Its true legacy was method: net-native aesthetic tribalism, slowed tempos as mood, and typography-as-brand — all inherited by vaporwave and the dark-synth diaspora.",
