    cat: "Music & Sonic Culture", c: "outdoor bass dance culture", e: "community_subculture", o: "Kingston street dances built around portable PA rigs; carried to Britain by Windrush-era migration",
    g: "Jamaica (Kingston) and the UK (Notting Hill, Birmingham, Leicester)", p: "1950s-present", era: "independence era to digital",
    sum: "Hand-built speaker stacks, exclusive dubplates and MC clashes — the street-bass institution that became reggae's beating heart.",
    desc: "When radios were scarce, Kingston's poorer districts danced to the first sound systems — carts of hand-built speaker boxes run by operators like Coxsone Dodd's Downbeat and Duke Reid the Trojan, later refined by King Tubby's bass innovations. The system made the music: exclusive dubplates cut for one crew, selectors controlling the night, and toasting DJs chatting over rhythms — the seedbed of rap and of dancehall itself. Migrating to Britain, the culture took root at Notting Hill Carnival and in Midlands clubs, with outfits like Saxon Studio International producing stars from Smiley Culture onward. Clash etiquette, vertical bass feel, box-spray gold lettering and bass-bin physics remain a living lineage running through jungle, dubstep, grime and every festival stage with a wall of scoops.",
    col: [{ h: "#17140f", n: "bass-bin black" }, { h: "#c9a53a", n: "glitter gold" }, { h: "#b0182a", n: "session crimson" }, { h: "#2a7d4f", n: "bottle green" }, { h: "#e8e2d2", n: "curtain cream" }],
