    cat: "Music & Sonic Culture", c: "hand-lettered event poster vernacular", e: "community_subculture", o: "Kingston promoters, sign painters and photocopy shops advertising sessions and clashes",
    g: "Jamaica — Kingston, Spanish Town", p: "1980s-2000s", era: "digital riddim era",
    sum: "Glitter-marker gradients, exploding letterforms and zinc-fence banners — the street graphics that advertised every Jamaican session.",
    desc: "Every Jamaican dance — from yard sessions to Boxing Day's Sting spectacular — needed walls of graphics, and a vernacular industry of sign painters, photocopy flyers and screen-printed banners supplied them. Names ballooned in outlined marker with glitter and metallic gradients; sound systems, selectors and promoters stacked in descending scale; stars, flashes and hand-drawn DJ caricatures burst from zinc fences and lamp posts. Photographers and documenters like Beth Lesser, who shot and wrote on the Kingston scene through the 80s, preserved the graphic ecology alongside the music. The style's exaggeration logic — maximum star power per square inch — migrated to UK and US reggae promotion and into digital riddim-era Photoshop flyers, where its DNA survives in today's bashment promos.",
    col: [{ h: "#f2c53d", n: "glitter gold" }, { h: "#d43a2f", n: "banner red" }, { h: "#2a9d5c", n: "marker green" }, { h: "#2b2530", n: "zinc-fence dusk" }, { h: "#f5f0e0", n: "photocopy white" }],
