    sum: "Shaved heads, Australian tracksuits and 200 BPM distortion — Rotterdam's working-class answer to Amsterdam's smiley house.",
    desc: "Gabber — Amsterdam slang for 'mate' — hardened into a full subculture in early-90s Rotterdam, where dockland youth built identity on the fastest, most distorted techno in Europe. The uniform was instantly readable: shaved heads for all genders, Nike Air Max, and the tracksuits of Australian, a sportswear brand adopted wholesale by the scene. The hakkuh dance pumped arms over kicking feet at 150-200 BPM; flyer and cassette art answered with chrome skulls, distorted Rotterdam Records logos and tram-stop typography. Mega-raves under ID&T's Thunderdome banner scaled it into stadiums while media panic and the genre's own extremity burned it out around the millennium. Its stubby afterlife lives in hardstyle, festival hardcore and Dutch pop's ironic affection for its own toughest decade.",
