    sum: "Cassette covers, wet-look flyers and club-bling graphics — the vernacular visual economy of reggaeton's perreo nights.",
    desc: "Before reggaeton had a global industry it had a flyer-and-cassette economy: San Juan's underground spread through DJ Playero's Playero mixtapes and The Noise compilations, copied to cassette and advertised on photocopied flyers whose wet-look gradients, sparkled logos and posed-crew photography set the template. Perreo — the dance and the night itself — supplied the visual logic: maximum energy, bodies front and centre, gold and neon over photocopy blue. As the 2000s boom hit, Luny Tunes' Mas Flow (2003) era branded the sound with glossy urban-latin graphics, diamond type and video-vixen glamour, which today's urbano superstars retool through streaming cover art and Instagram promo. The vernacular remains proudly low-fi at street level even as its centre goes platinum.",
