    sum: "Crime-scene melodrama, Palladium ballroom flash and bold latin modernism — the sleeve art of Nueva York's hottest rhythm.",
    desc: "Fania Records turned salsa into a brand, and its covers taught the world how the music looked: Willie Colon's early albums posed the teenage bandleader as mobster beside Hector Lavoe — El Malo (1967) launched the crime-scene idiom, continued through Cosa Nuestra's vintage car and autopsy-table imagery. Izzy Sanabria, who designed for Fania and published Latin New York magazine, pushed bold lettering, poster-like illustration and stage-photo spreads that unified the scene's look from record sleeve to club flyer. The Fania All-Stars' Live at the Cheetah (1972) and the Siembra (1978) collaboration of Blades and Colon supplied the canon's peak images: baroque, urban, proud and sometimes violent. The corpus now anchors latin-design collecting and the salsa dura revival's branding.",
    col: [{ h: "#8e1b1b", n: "crimson bolero" }, { h: "#d9a52a", n: "brass bandleader gold" }, { h: "#20242c", n: "downtown night navy" }, { h: "#e8ddc4", n: "ticket cream" }, { h: "#3f6e54", n: "mojito green" }],
