    g: "Nigeria — Lagos", p: "1970s-1990s", era: "afrobeat seventies-eighties",
    sum: "Hand-painted Fela covers that were political cartoons — grotesque generals, Pidgin slogans and riotous color against military rule.",
    desc: "Afrobeat's albums were arguments, and their covers were painted arguments: from Alagbon Close (1974) onward, self-taught painter Lemi Ghariokwu created 26 sleeves for Fela Kuti, working from the music and from long conversations at Kalakuta Republic. Zombie (1976) caricatured soldiers as vacant clockwork; Beasts of No Nation (1989) turned UN bigwigs into snarling monsters, captioned in English, Pidgin and Yoruba lettering that functioned as on-record protest under censorship. Ghariokwu's grotesque airbrush-and-acrylic style — bulging eyes, banner text, checkerboard satire — made each LP a pamphlet you could play, and Lagos studios, printers and fans turned the covers into street-level political education. The tradition's lineage runs through Nigerian modern design collecting and today's Afrobeats sleeve revivals quoting his hand outright.",
